    "id": "financial-resilience-prime-contract-cash-flow",  
    "title": "Financial Resilience: Can Your Cash Flow Support a Prime Contract?",  
    "excerpt": "Prime contracting breaks companies on capital structure. If you cannot finance the Prime Float with bonded capacity and DCAA-ready accounting, you are one invoice away from default.",  
    "date": "2026-03-30",  
    "readTime": "12 min",  
    "thumbnail": "https://cdn.marblism.com/20IMrglb1l-.webp",  
    "content": "<p class=\"lead-paragraph\">Prime contracting does not break companies on capability. It breaks them on capital structure. The failure mode is predictable: you mobilize like a prime, you carry payroll and subs like a prime, and you get paid like a receivable lender. In 2026, invoice approval is a control event. Payment integrity scrutiny turns “submit” into “prove.” If you cannot finance the Prime Float with bonded capacity, committed credit, disciplined working capital, and DCAA-ready accounting, you are not prime-ready. You are one invoice return away from default.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Executive Brief</h2>\\n<p><strong>Definition (BOBG):</strong> the <strong>Prime Float</strong> is the funded gap between <strong>prime-level cash-out</strong> (mobilization, payroll, subcontractors, insurance, reporting, compliance) and <strong>cash-in</strong> (accepted invoice payment). Prime Float starts the day you incur performance costs. It ends only when invoice acceptance and payment become predictable.</p>\\n\\n<p><strong>Prime contract financial readiness = 6 tests:</strong></p>\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Bonding capacity:</strong> capacity sized to the opportunity.</li>\\n<li><strong>Committed credit:</strong> LOC availability that survives covenant tests.</li>\\n<li><strong>Working capital math:</strong> liquidity to bridge Net-30/60 federal payments.</li>\\n<li><strong>DCAA-compliant accounting:</strong> job-costing and billing traceability.</li>\\n<li><strong>Risk management:</strong> insurance and liability controls.</li>\\n<li><strong>Growth-trap controls:</strong> governance that blocks award volume from outpacing cash.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<p><strong>BOBG rule:</strong> if you cannot fund <strong>90 days</strong> of prime burn without a single government payment, you do not accept the award.</p>"  
