    "content": "<p class=\"lead-paragraph\"><strong>Evaluators do not “review” proposals. They execute a scoring workflow under time pressure.</strong> Your document is not being “read.” It is being processed against Section M, inside page limits, with an audit trail in mind.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Definitive stance</h2>\\n<p><strong>You do not win by being compliant.</strong> You win by being <strong>compliantly obvious</strong>. If an evaluator can’t quote you, they won’t credit you.</p>\\n\\n<h2>What Win Theme Architecture really is</h2>\\n<p><strong>Win Theme Architecture is scoring engineering.</strong> It converts your capability into evaluator language that fits Section M, survives consensus, and holds up in a protest file.</p>\\n\\n<h3>The evaluator constraint you must design for</h3>\\n<p><strong>Section L constrains what evaluators are allowed to see.</strong> Section M constrains what they are allowed to credit.</p>\\n<p>If your proof is not under the exact M-mirrored heading the evaluator must write up, the evaluator must infer. <strong>Inference is protest risk.</strong> The safe move is a downgrade.</p>"  
