    "excerpt": "DHA will award to vendors that prove measurable superiority in EHR integration, cyber resilience, and interoperability—fast, with evidence.",  
    "date": "2026-03-30",  
    "readTime": "10 min",  
    "thumbnail": "https://cdn.marblism.com/FuKX4WFkUfb.webp",  
    "content": "<p class=\"lead-paragraph\"><strong>By Aeron Carter</strong> — The Defense Health Agency (DHA) is done paying for “good enough” health IT. In 2026, technical superiority is the win condition. Delivered capability. The next wave of DHA buying will reward contractors that can prove EHR integration, cybersecurity resilience, and data interoperability.</p>\\n\\n<h2>What “Technical Superiority” Means to DHA (2026)</h2>\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>EHR integration:</strong> Safe workflow adoption and stable interfaces.</li>\\n<li><strong>Cybersecurity:</strong> Evidence-driven ATO support and Zero Trust alignment.</li>\\n<li><strong>Interoperability:</strong> Standards-based exchange (HL7/FHIR) and data trust.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<p><strong>BOBG truth:</strong> The “best” technical solution loses if it cannot be <strong>operated</strong> inside DHA constraints. Technical superiority must be auditable.</p>"  
